00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:58,310
more physics, which is the work of Jörg Teschner. So there are lots of other contributions

10
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:05,120
that should be mentioned, but I'm not the right one to explain them. So I'm going to

11
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:11,280
begin by reviewing the gauge theory approach to geometric line lines from my work with

12
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:16,940
Anton Kapustin a number of years ago. And that'll be the starting point where we'll

13
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:20,660
see how to make a new twist that will lead us to the current developments in the work

14
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:27,240
of Etingof, Frenkel, and Kasia. So the starting point for geometric Langlands is a certain

15
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:35,890
four-dimensional gauge theory that has a gauge group G, or it has a Langlands, Goddard-Noitz-Allof

16
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:44,130
dual group G-dull. I'll use this symbol for the dual group. And this theory, okay, obviously

17
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:52,040
I'll tell you, say a number of things that are very schematic, because if you're not

